# Commits that changed formatting without changing meaning.
#
# `git blame` on these files would otherwise point at the reflow rather than at
# whoever wrote the sentence -- and these documents carry *why it is like that*,
# which is the part worth attributing.
#
# It does nothing until you turn it on, which is the half people miss:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Per clone, because it is a local preference rather than a property of the
# history. GitHub honours the file automatically; `git blame` on the command
# line does not until the line above is run.
#
# One hash per line, longest-lived first, with a comment saying what it did.

# docs: unwrap every doc -- prose on one line per paragraph, no hard wraps
# (hash added in the follow-up commit, because it cannot be known until the
#  commit it names exists)
